        "text": "Based on market psychology theories, an asset's price tends to change consistently in response to the overall market sentiment, which is also dynamic. How do emotions change during market cycles? All markets go through cycles of expansion and contraction. When a market is in an expansion phase or bull market, there is a climate of optimism, belief and greed. Typically, these are the main emotions that lead to a strong buying activity. It's quite common to see a sort of cyclical or retroactive effect during market cycles. For example, the sentiment gets more positive as the prices go up, which then causes the sentiment to get even more positive, driving the market even higher. When the market starts to turn the other way, the euphoric mood can quickly turn into complacency as many traders refuse to believe that the uptrend is over. As prices continue to decline, the market's sentiment quickly moves to the negative side. It often includes feelings of anxiety, denial and panic.",
        "start": 400,
        "end": 59680,
        "confidence": 0.9857427,
        "channel": null,
